            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Life and Other Inventions</strong><br>\nIn later life, Watt's inventive spirit continued as he worked on devices such as the letter-copying machine, which was the precursor to the modern photocopier. His lesser-known inventions reflect a diverse and ingenious mind.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Character</strong><br>\nWatt was known for being modest about his considerable achievements. He had a close circle of intellectual friends, including Erasmus Darwin and Joseph Priestley, with whom he discussed scientific and technological advancements.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Watt's Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nJames Watt died in 1819, leaving behind inventions that revolutionized industry and engineering. His work paved the way for the modern world, and he is remembered as one of the most influential figures of the Industrial Revolution.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
